-- A sale that already happened, reported to us afterwards.
--
-- Generated by `pnpm --filter @vyloop/db db:generate` and reviewed by hand. It
-- is the only SQL this feature ships.
--
-- ## The hole this table closes
--
-- The POS accumulates points for the one merchant that already has an
-- integration, because that integration sends `points` already computed. There
-- is no channel for the other merchants, and there is no channel for a sale
-- that a *different* system completed. So a customer can register, be handed a
-- pass, and then never accumulate on it: the pass is a document with no effect.
--
-- This table is that channel, and it is deliberately the *inverse* of the flow
-- the spec used to describe. That earlier version had us create the order in
-- Mercado Pago (`POST /v1/orders`) with our checkin's UUID as
-- `external_reference`, and the cashier paying through our screen. **We do not
-- own the checkout.** A merchant's terminal completes the sale and reports the
-- fact; we accumulate on a fact we did not create.
--
-- The product reason is larger than the implementation one: loyalty that
-- requires possessing the checkout only grows at the rate of merchants who
-- install our POS, which is the rate we are trying to escape. Loyalty that
-- listens to the terminal grows at the rate of all of them.
--
-- ## Why `unique(business_id, payment_id)` and not anything else
--
-- Because the event is already named when it reaches us, and named by the thing
-- that knows it best. The payment rail's own transaction id is available at the
-- exact moment the money moves, so idempotency costs nothing to obtain:
--
--   terminal retries the same payment -> same payment_id -> already seen
--   customer buys twice               -> two payment_ids -> accumulates twice
--
-- The rejected alternatives, and why they each lose one of those two cases:
--
--   A. Dedupe on the customer + a time window. A window cannot distinguish "the
--      terminal retried" from "the customer bought again", so it swallows one
--      of them. It has to pick, and either choice is a merchant complaint.
--   B. Dedupe on an `order_id` stored after we created the order. That is the
--      other product -- our POS creating the order -- which is a later stage
--      with a real hole between creating an order and someone paying it. This
--      stage does not create orders, so it has no order id to key on.
--   C. A partial index, `WHERE payment_id IS NOT NULL`. Correct in Postgres,
--      because `NULL != NULL` means the manual path's NULL never collides, so
--      the partial index would gain nothing and hide that reasoning one layer
--      down. A plain unique constraint is used instead and the reasoning lives
--      in `schema.ts` where the column is declared.
--
-- ## Why there is no `products` table
--
-- Because the identity of a product belongs to the merchant. Copying it here
-- would be maintaining a copy that becomes a lie the moment they rename
-- something. A catalogue we own drifts from the merchant's reality at exactly
-- the speed their business changes, and every statistic built on it would be
-- quietly wrong. `items` is therefore a `jsonb` bag of the merchant's own
-- description, kept as a snapshot of what was sold, and never as a source of
-- truth.
--
-- `amount_cents` is authoritative and `items` is best-effort. A payment rail
-- transports the *total*, not the cart, so `items` will very often be NULL --
-- that is the expectation to hold, not a defect to fix. If the two ever
-- disagree, the money wins and the sale is never refused over metadata.
--
-- ## Why `member_code` is stored next to the resolved `customer_id`
--
-- Because the resolution is not permanent. Codes get reassigned and customer
-- rows get withdrawn, so a row carrying only `customer_id` cannot answer "what
-- did the terminal actually send" months later -- which is the question a
-- merchant asks when they do not believe an attribution. The scanned string is
-- the audit trail; the uuid is the current answer. Both, deliberately.
--
-- ## Why `points` is nullable, and why that is the design rather than a gap
--
-- `NULL` means "the sale is recorded and the credit has not landed yet", and it
-- is *observable*: `WHERE points IS NULL` is a backlog somebody can find and
-- finish. `NOT NULL DEFAULT 0` would have made the failure state
-- unrepresentable and therefore invisible, which is the same thing as losing the
-- customer's money quietly.
--
-- `POST /v1/checkins` depends on this. It inserts with `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`
-- on `(business_id, payment_id)`; when the row is already there it reads
-- `points`, and a retry *completes* a credit that a partial failure left
-- pending. A retry that could only ever answer "already done" would turn every
-- transient error into a customer who paid and never accumulated.
--
-- ## Why `business_id` is the tenant, cascading from `tenants.id`
--
-- It comes from the gateway credential and never from the body. `member_code` is
-- unique per business (`uq_customers_business_member_code`), so the credential's
-- scope is the only thing stopping one terminal from resolving another merchant's
-- pass. Both foreign keys cascade: deleting a merchant must not leave sales rows
-- pointing at nothing, and a sale is not a record that outlives the shop that
-- made it.
--
-- ## Not applied here
--
-- `drizzle-kit migrate` against production is forbidden in this repository: the
-- ledger has drift and running the migrator would apply migrations twice. A
-- person applies this by hand, after review. This file is left on disk, unrun.
CREATE TABLE "checkins" (
	"id" uuid PRIMARY KEY DEFAULT gen_random_uuid() NOT NULL,
	"business_id" uuid NOT NULL,
	"customer_id" uuid NOT NULL,
	"member_code" text NOT NULL,
	"payment_id" text,
	"amount_cents" integer NOT NULL,
	"items" jsonb,
	"note" text,
	"points" integer,
	"created_at" timestamp with time zone DEFAULT now() NOT NULL,
	CONSTRAINT "uq_checkins_business_payment" UNIQUE("business_id","payment_id")
);
